comments[4]{created_at,author,text}:
  "2026-11-21T11:55:06.687Z","harness:claude-code","Implementation on branch fix/history-remedies-converge, 2026-11-21. In src/sdk/history/salvage.ts, inspectHistoryTail now separates the line scan (scanSalvageLines) from the chain judgment (judgeRetainedChain). Discarding an invalid suffix still requires a nonempty hash-verified prefix. Removing only lines that cannot hold a record (JSON null, boolean or number) no longer requires the chain to verify, because removal conceals nothing. The retained records then replay leniently for the id check and the ownership fallback, and the history_salvage record keeps the newest epoch a record declares, or none for an implicit stream. The result reports the real verdict: chain_drift_before, verify_ok and verify_errors, which were hard-coded to false, true and [] before. A history_chain_unverified warning names pm history-repair <id> --append-only --dry-run. A record-less stream (only scalar lines) still refuses. A broken chain with nothing to discard refuses with a next step naming the append-only repair preview. pm health then reports the stream as a bridgeable history_drift_chain_mismatch (pm-3os5p6), and the advised append-only repair bridges the break with the salvaged stream as a byte prefix. Proven in tests/integration/lifecycle/history-remedies.integration.spec.ts (the null line plus the bridgeable break, then salvage, append-only repair, a clean history_drift check and history --verify --strict-exit), together with the suffix refusal, the nothing-to-discard next step and the implicit-epoch case."
  "2026-11-21T12:39:42.804Z","harness:claude-code","Review round 1 on PR #1763, 2026-11-21. Fixed: (a) salvage checks the item identity at every replayed state, so a misfiled stream that names another item refuses even when a later delete cleared the id. (b) The salvage record is planned before the lock, so a preview refuses wherever the write would; a digest algorithm this pm cannot compute refuses typed with history_salvage_refused and the upgrade step. (c) The follow-up warning names the append-only preview and the rewriting preview for a break a bridge cannot repair. (d) Lenient replay skips a root replacement that leaves no item object instead of throwing, in replay.ts lenientApplyReplayPatch, which the rewriting repair shares. (e) A stream of only scalar lines salvages to an empty stream without a salvage record, and for a live item the warning names pm history-repair <id>, which rebuilds it (pm-3os5p6)."
  "2026-11-21T13:58:37.012Z","harness:claude-code","Review rounds 2 to 4 on PR #1763, 2026-11-21. Fixed in the shared lenient replay (replay.ts lenientApplyReplayPatch), which salvage and the rewriting repair both use. A patch may pass through a non-object root and restore the item. A patch that ends without an item root is a deletion when its entry is a delete, and replays as the canonical empty document. Any other such patch is replayed again, skipping only the ops that would leave no root, so the ops that did apply are kept. Salvage judges a verified chain's identity on its strict replay states, plans its record only when something will be removed, and gives restore advice for a malformed digest label and upgrade advice only for a well-formed unknown one."
  "2026-11-21T15:24:56.728Z","harness:claude-code","Review round 8 (CodeRabbit on 43c44786b), 2026-11-21: the salvage record is built from the canonical empty document, keeping its empty patch and the last kept record's anchors. Unverified legacy metadata that does not canonicalize, for example metadata.comments set to {}, therefore no longer throws before the scalar line is removed. Regression test: 'salvages an unverified legacy stream whose metadata does not canonicalize' in tests/integration/lifecycle/history-remedies.integration.spec.ts."
